 else would be inclined  to do there was sometimes a point that  they didn't want me to go beyond and  then there'd be a behind-the-scenes  negotiations a little bit of pushback  and you kind of settled somewhere in the  middle sometimes what do you think about  Los Angeles getting the Olympics  and uh if the travel ban with a  president Trump how that is going to  impact this vote since they're gonna be  voting on it now but then you're gonna  have yeah you know we it's not you know  for what twenty twenty four people think  it's far down the road and it is seven  years down the road but the vote the  decision just around the corner and we  can only guess there's so many different  constituencies within the IOC we can  only guess how some of them will feel  about American policies and and  president Trump's initiatives and what  effect that might have on Los Angeles's  chances I'll say this from where I said  Los Angeles is probably the city best  situated to pull it off because it has  so many usable venues spread all around  LA and now add to that no matter what  you think a Stan Kroenke leaving st.  Louis and building his new sports Mecca  in in the Los Angeles area that adds  more facilities so I think they're  better situated to pull it off than the  other cities that are bidding for it but  there's always politics involved yeah I  was told that they might swap it and  have Paris go before Los Angeles because  of the travel ban and and voting on it  have you heard anything and what do you  think about that possibility well and  again I don't want to speak out of my  depth here but Paris has issues of its  own one issue is not the setting itself  spectacular to have an Olympics in Paris  and have the Eiffel Tower and the  schoenflies a and the Louvre and all the  rest as some of the backdrops but they  have had their own problems with  terrorism and indigenous terrorists  there may be more of them per capita in  some European cities and there are in  the United States and security is an  ever-growing concern so that's going to  be among among the things the questions  I guess that those who represent Paris  will have to answer for the IOC so I  really it's a jumble to me and I guess  there's pluses and minuses on either  side of it your advice for Mike Tirico  have you given him or  will you give him any advice taking over  the Olympic throne you know we've spent  a good deal of time together especially  lately and of course the first bit of  advice is the same bit of advice that  every one of us got when we started in  the business no matter what we wanted to  do just be yourself  and of course you can't just be yourself  until you pretty much mastered robotic  li the basics of the business and that's  when a personal style and a personal  touch begins to emerge just be yourself  is the easiest device to give and one of  the hardest things to do and it only  comes when you become comfortable and  confident enough to let your own  personality come out but I think the one  bit of solid advice that I've been able  to give them is it's important to know  what you don't need to know and after a  couple of Olympics it dawned on me you  know what no matter how hard you study  no matter how dedicated you are there's  no way you can know every platform diver  from Bolivia or every cross-country  skier from Finland and why should you  try the role of the host and Jim McKay  was the guy who pretty much set the  template for it the role of the host is  to be a good generalist to really know  the history of the Olympics to really  have some perspective on it  to know a lot about the host city and  the host nation and to know a lot about  the two dozen or so stories and athletes  that are likely to be the focus in  primetime and then something comes onto  the radar screen unexpectedly the  researchers are terrific and you have to  be able to take a briefing quickly and  make a narrative out of it even if you  didn't expect that to be 